  "worked_solution": "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int 3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(3t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(3t)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(3(0))+C \\\\ 1 & =-1+C \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is given by $v(t)=2-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(3t).$\n\nNow, we integrate the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (2-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(3t))\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2t-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(3t)+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $K,$ we use the fact that $x(0)=5.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =2(0)-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(3(0))+K \\\\ K & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=2t-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(3t)+5.\n$$",
